      "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"a-field-guide-to-hlc\">A field guide to HLC\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>Holographic Linguistic Cosmography, or HLC, is a way of thinking about language models through the cultural material from which they are made and the practices through which people use them. Its recurring image is the telescope: an instrument makes some patterns visible while its construction limits what can be seen. This small interconnected guide introduces that framework, its working vocabulary, and its internal criticisms.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The starting distinction is between the \u003ca data-page-link href=\"foundations--cultural-hologram.html\">cultural hologram\u003c/a>, the \u003ca data-page-link href=\"foundations--digital-trace.html\">digital trace\u003c/a>, and the \u003ca data-page-link href=\"foundations--latent-manifold.html\">latent manifold\u003c/a>. Culture exceeds its records; records exceed any particular training corpus; a model is shaped by that selected material without becoming a complete archive of it. Keeping those differences visible is more useful than taking the astronomical imagery literally.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"enter-from-a-question\">Enter from a question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\u003cli>What does the name mean? Begin with \u003ca data-page-link href=\"start--what-is-hlc.html\">What is HLC?\u003c/a>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>How might I use it? Try the \u003ca data-page-link href=\"practice--telescope-protocol.html\">telescope protocol\u003c/a>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>What could go wrong? Read \u003ca data-page-link href=\"critique--novelty-and-evidence.html\">Novelty needs evidence\u003c/a> and \u003ca data-page-link href=\"critique--recursive-loops.html\">When reflection becomes a loop\u003c/a>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Where does the metaphor stop? Visit \u003ca data-page-link href=\"foundations--language-and-reality.html\">Language and reality\u003c/a> or \u003ca data-page-link href=\"extensions--multimodal.html\">Beyond text\u003c/a>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The pages form several \u003ca data-page-link href=\"start--reading-paths.html\">reading paths\u003c/a>, but there is no single required sequence. The links deliberately connect methods with their limits: a page about generating an unfamiliar concept leads to questions about evidence, and a page about changing lenses leads to the problem of shared assumptions. A useful map should expose those crossings.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This guide is an original explanatory adaptation of the local HLC notes, primarily from November 2025. It is neither a replacement edition of those notes nor a claim that their proposals constitute an established scientific theory. Some source passages speak more confidently about model geometry and cognition than their evidence supports; this edition preserves the ideas while identifying interpretation and speculation. The \u003ca data-page-link href=\"sources.html\">Sources and editorial approach\u003c/a> page records the source paths and the adaptation boundary.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Read a page, follow a connection, and return with a different question. The goal is a usable vocabulary for inquiry, with enough friction to keep a compelling explanation from becoming its own proof.\u003c/p>",
